00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:04.851
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Hi,
I'm Maya Kaczorowski,

00:00:04.851 --> 00:00:06.572
and we're here KubeCon
EU in Copenhagen.

00:00:06.572 --> 00:00:08.280
And I'm here with
Jason Yee from Datadog.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:08.850
How are you?

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:10.080
JASON YEE: Hey, I'm
doing all right.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.970
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: So tell
me what Datadog does.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:14.261
JASON YEE: Yeah, we're a
SAS-based monitoring platform.

00:00:14.261 --> 00:00:17.010
So we help customers collect
their metrics, infrastructure

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:21.060
metrics, application metrics,
traces, so thinking of APM,

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.960
and their logs,
all in one place.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:23.580
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
And so how do I

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.442
use that to look at
my Kubernetes metrics?

00:00:25.442 --> 00:00:27.900
JASON YEE: Yeah, so we have
Kubernetes integrations already

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:28.420
built in.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.780
So whether you're running
your Kubernetes in GKE

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.450
or if you're running it just
on your own standalone on-prem

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.740
stuff, all of that metrics can
be gathered up and sent to us

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:39.780
so you can see it
all in one place.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:40.450
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
That's super cool.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:40.835
JASON YEE: Yeah.

00:00:40.835 --> 00:00:42.390
It makes it really
handy for people

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:46.016
that are in that
hybrid cloud situation

00:00:46.016 --> 00:00:47.640
where they're just
playing around with,

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.098
maybe they need
some stuff on-prem,

00:00:49.098 --> 00:00:51.190
maybe they're
migrating to the cloud,

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:53.670
it's really helpful to have
everything in one place

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:54.690
as you're doing that.

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:57.300
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Single pane
of glass, we totally get that.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:01.690
Anything else you want to talk
about about Datadog and Google?

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:04.050
JASON YEE: We love Google.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:04.650
Yeah.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:05.970
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: That's
always good to hear.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:08.303
JASON YEE: It's been really
great to partner with Google

00:01:08.303 --> 00:01:11.160
on not only integrations
on technical things,

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.260
but also with events
because Google

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:16.770
has a bunch of great events like
Google Cloud Next and Google

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:17.460
IO.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.168
So it's always nice
to be a part of those

00:01:19.168 --> 00:01:21.690
and see the Google ecosystem
growing, and having

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.460
more people there
who are obviously

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:26.865
adopting container technology,
Kubernetes, things like that.

00:01:26.865 --> 00:01:28.240
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
And so where do

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.110
I go to find out more about
running Datadog on GKE?

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.444
JASON YEE: Yeah, you can find
out more at DatadogHQ.com.

00:01:33.444 --> 00:01:35.193
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Are
you using Kubernetes

00:01:35.193 --> 00:01:36.430
to monitor Kubernetes?

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:37.180
JASON YEE: We are.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:40.500
So a big project
for us in this year

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.500
is trying to shift all
of our applications

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.054
to be containerized.

00:01:45.054 --> 00:01:46.970
It helps us be a little
bit more standardized.

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:48.690
It helps us to roll
out wherever we

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.960
need to without
the fear of things

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.390
being fragile when they're
in virtual machines

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.430
or they're just
actually on servers.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.500
So as we're rolling
out, Kubernetes

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.780
is a big plan, or
a big part of that

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.760
because it just makes managing
containers so much easier.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:04.260
So yeah.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:07.680
We help our customers who are
running Kubernetes monitor

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.259
their Kubernetes, but it's also
all running on our Kubernetes.

00:02:11.259 --> 00:02:13.050
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
Turtles all the way down.

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:13.520
JASON YEE: It is.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.020
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
Thank you so much.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:15.930
JASON YEE: Thanks.

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:18.471
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: And thank you
